  "headline": "Everyone's Friend, Nobody's Ally",
  "regional_lead": "The Hormuz crisis has not forced the Near East's key powers to choose sides — it has rewarded those who refuse to. Saudi Arabia, the UAE, and Turkey each ran contradictory tracks simultaneously this week and extracted leverage from the contradiction. Pakistan tried the same and failed. The difference is not moral but operational: ambiguity held only where the underlying relationships were strong enough to carry it.\n\nSaudi Arabia offers the clearest illustration. Volodymyr Zelensky arrived in Jeddah for his second visit in a month and left with a security arrangement — Ukrainian air-defence expertise for Saudi financial and diplomatic backing. In the same week, Reuters confirmed Saudi imports of Russian fuel oil surged 18% in March to 1m metric tons, bought cheaply to free up Saudi crude for Red Sea export, while Saudi Aramco was in reported talks to buy interceptor drones from Ukrainian manufacturers to protect oil fields Iran had explicitly threatened. Backing Ukraine, buying Russian energy, mediating with Iran: Riyadh ran all three tracks at once without apparent embarrassment. The traffic into Jeddah confirmed that the kingdom has made itself the essential go-between in the Hormuz crisis precisely because it will talk to everyone. Ajit Doval, India's national security adviser, arrived on Narendra Modi's orders for a one-day surprise visit; Prince Faisal bin Farhan, the foreign minister, worked through calls with counterparts in Iran, Qatar, Pakistan, Oman, Kuwait, Bahrain, and beyond.\n\nThe UAE carried the same logic further. It emerged this week that Israel had secretly deployed an Iron Dome battery, with several dozen operators, to the UAE during the war — the first time the system had been used operationally outside Israel or the United States, activated after a call between Benjamin Netanyahu and Mohammed bin Zayed. Israeli soldiers were running a weapons system on Emirati soil during active hostilities, and the UAE chose to disclose this now, in the ceasefire window, signalling the depth of what the Abraham Accords built. The disclosure came as Abu Dhabi signed a formal bilateral framework with Yvette Cooper, Britain's foreign secretary, covering defence, artificial intelligence, energy, and illicit finance, and as Mr bin Zayed spoke to counterparts in the United States, Russia, Britain, the Netherlands, Canada, and Finland. In the same week, UAE officials privately warned Washington that dollar shortages could force oil sales in Chinese yuan — leverage, not distress, given the country's $285bn in reserves — while Mansoor Abulhoul, the UAE ambassador to Britain, went on BBC Radio 4 to defend the arrest of 375 people, including British nationals, for sharing footage of Iranian attacks online. The result is a country using every instrument at once: deepening its Western alliances, pressing Washington, deploying Israeli hardware, and managing the information environment at home.\n\nPakistan is the exception that sharpens the argument. It had positioned itself as the sole broker between Washington and Tehran — the White House's own phrase — and when Iran refused to hold direct talks on Pakistani soil, the mediation unravelled in public. Inter-Services Public Relations, the army's media arm, made things worse by issuing a communiqué claiming American negotiators were already in-country, a claim international outlets quickly corrected. Iran's state media accused Pakistan of playing a double game. The damage was partially repaired: Iran's foreign minister returned to Islamabad, Tehran sent a revised proposal through the Pakistani channel, and Iran's president thanked the army chief by name in a 50-minute call. But Pakistan had been demoted from sole mediator to one contested channel among several — Iran's side trip to Moscow had ended the monopoly. Parliament remained prorogued throughout; the National Assembly had not sat since April 8, meaning the country's most consequential diplomatic episode in years unfolded without a single day of legislative oversight. What the false communiqué revealed is not incompetence alone but the fragility of an institution performing confidence it does not fully possess. Turkey's trajectory that week ran the other way. Hakan Fidan, the foreign minister, spent two days in London signing a strategic partnership with Ms Cooper, then announced that Turkey stood ready to send forces into the Hormuz Strait to clear mines after any deal — shifting Ankara from facilitator to potential participant in any post-settlement arrangement — while calling Iran's foreign minister, Pakistan's counterpart, and American mediators within the same week. Where Pakistan's multiple relationships buckled under scrutiny, Turkey's held, because Mr Fidan had built them carefully and claimed no monopoly.\n\nIndia is arriving late and moving fast. Mr Doval flew to Riyadh and Abu Dhabi within eight days, on the prime minister's orders, at a moment when 14 Indian vessels were stranded in the Hormuz region and some 9m to 10m Indian nationals lived in Gulf states at risk. Ahead of the Heads of Mission conference opening April 28, where all Indian envoys will be briefed on the crisis, Delhi is treating the Gulf not as a region to monitor but as a theatre where Indian interests must be actively managed. The logic is identical to Riyadh's and Abu Dhabi's: with 135 ships crossing the strait each day before the war, and that figure now sharply lower, neutrality is too expensive. The countries that built their positions before the crisis — Saudi Arabia by becoming the essential go-between, the UAE by deepening every available alliance at once — are collecting the returns. India's preparations for the India-Africa Forum Summit, the first in a decade, scheduled for late May, and the formal UAE strategic defence partnership being put in place quickly, suggest Delhi has understood where influence is being built and is trying to catch up. Whether it can do so without Pakistan's mistakes — overreaching, being caught out, losing the monopoly — is what the next few weeks will show.",
  "gap_paragraphs": [
    "Notably absent this week: Iran's internal power struggle (ISW confirms IRGC Commander Vahidi has prevailed) should be producing differentiated regional responses: states with de-escalation interests (Saudi Arabia, UAE) recalibrating their diplomatic architectures while states with mediation roles (Turkey, Pakistan) reassessing whether their counterpart has decision-making authority. The ISW Vahidi assessment — if correct — is the most important single piece of information for evaluating every regional mediation effort this week, and it is being processed in only one of five country desks. If Vahidi is blocking ceasefire diplomacy and controls the IRGC, then Turkey's Hormuz demining offer, Pakistan's 'amended framework' transmission, and Saudi Arabia's FM consultation blitz are all being absorbed by an Iranian decision-making structure that may be structurally unresponsive to them. This is a collection gap: the regional framework predicts that Iranian internal power dynamics should be the primary variable shaping all mediation assessments, and it is largely absent from four of five country analyses.",
    "Notably absent this week: UAE's Iron Dome deployment revelation — confirming IDF personnel operated on UAE soil during active hostilities — should be producing visible diplomatic management responses in Saudi Arabia and India, both of which have complex relationships with Israel and are positioned as Gulf energy security partners. The Iron Dome asymmetry is analytically significant: it reveals that UAE's security integration with Israel is operationally deeper than Saudi Arabia's, which has structural implications for Gulf security competition during any future Iran confrontation. That neither the SA nor IN desks processed this finding suggests either a collection gap (the J-Post reporting had not reached those country analysts) or a deliberate analytical decision to treat it as a UAE-specific finding. Given the structural implications for regional security architecture, it warrants cross-desk visibility.",
    "Notably absent this week: Turkey's Eurofighter negotiations with the UK — the documented purpose of the Turkey-UK Strategic Partnership Framework — should be producing visible reactions or recalibrations in the Greek-Cypriot diplomatic track and in US CAATSA posture, given that Eurofighter delivery to Turkey remains contested on arms export grounds. The absence may reflect genuine quiet on this front — Greece and Cyprus may not have publicly reacted to a UK-Turkey framework, and CAATSA enforcement is a slow-moving bureaucratic process. But the gap is worth flagging because the Eurofighter dimension of the Turkey-UK framework is the development most likely to generate downstream consequences in NATO alliance dynamics, and those consequences are not visible in any country report this week."
  ],
  "card_summary": "Saudi Arabia, the UAE, and Turkey extracted leverage this week by talking to all sides at once, while Pakistan's claim to be the sole US-Iran broker collapsed after its military falsely announced American negotiators were already in Islamabad."
}
